{"parentUuid":"d3967501-5ad0-4381-aae9-ed94145b9b6a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdo8T7wcDJi8DcUqVZsjR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So this \"worker\" called LaunchAgent watch-tabs is the active component I'm looking for—it's not literally named \"YouTube Guard,\" but it's the only candidate running with logs showing \"YoutubeFocusGuard\" and \"SocialMediaFocusGuard\" as previous names. The key thing is it blocks specific creators by name rather than YouTube as a whole during daytime hours. Right now the system maintains a list of creators it's blocking, and it runs constantly (START_HOUR=0, END_HOUR=24). But Matthieu wants something different: block all of youtube.com during daytime (10h-19h) with a \"Ask for time\" system that gives him decreasing time slots—5 minutes, then 4, 3, 2, 1, then nothing for the rest of the day. Instead of waiting X minutes before access opens, he wants an immediate button that grants time right away, shrinking with each click until he's out of allowances. This is a pretty significant shift from the current approach of blocking specific creators with fixed wait times and rewards. I need to implement full YouTube blocking during those hours and replace the wait-and-reward model with a daily countdown system that resets each morning. Since this is self-imposed strictness—Matthieu blocking himself—it's not a safety concern, just modifying his own code.\n\nFor the new \"YouTube Daytime\" flow, I'm creating a separate feature that blocks all youtube.com URLs between 10h-19h without touching the existing BLOCKED_PATTERNS. I'll track daily grant usage in a file that resets each day, where the first \"Ask for time\" grants 5 minutes immediately, the next gives 4, then 3, 2, 1, and after that it's done for the day. When time runs out, he goes straight back to the block page with the button still active as long as there are remaining grants.\n\nI need a de